\begin{proof}
Put \(m=\min_i\eta_i(y)\), \(M_a=\max_i\eta_i(y)\). The canonical interval \([m,M_a]\) contains zero and has width \(\Delta\le D_0\). Inherited prestage mixing gives lifts \(\psi_i\in[m,M_a]\); inherited synchronization gives output lifts \(b_i=\psi_i+\delta_i\) in the same interval. Hence
\begin{equation}
\re F_1(z(y))=\rho_1\cos b_1
\ge a_*\cos\Delta\ge a_*\cos D_0>0.
\label{eq:anchorhalfplane}
\end{equation}
The continuous right-half-plane argument is therefore a single global branch, and \(\vartheta=b_1\in[m,M_a]\). Nonvanishing alone would not justify that branch. In particular \(\vartheta=\psi_1+\delta_1\): it includes the prestage phase change and is not generally only the kick.

The output relative phases are \(b_i-b_1\in[-D_0,D_0]\). The principal arcsines in \eqref{eq:jointimage} recover these differences exactly, so
\begin{equation}
\eta_i(Gy)=b_i-b_1,\qquad \theta_{i,n}=\theta_n+\eta_i(y_n)
\label{eq:ordinarychannels}
\end{equation}
propagates the inherited consistent ordinary lift. There is no later unresolved multiple of \(2\pi\). Now \(|\vartheta(y_n)|\le\Delta_n\le c^n\Delta_{\rm init}\) proves the absolute-series bound and \eqref{eq:limitingphase}. The same lift identifies the limit with the inherited \(\theta_\infty\). Both \(\theta_n\) and that limiting phase lie in \(I_n\), giving the stronger net bound \(|\theta_\infty-\theta_n|\le\Delta_n\), hence the second part of \eqref{eq:phasetails}.
\end{proof}

